# Companion paper. preferred-citation stays the primary result (the measurement);
# this is the case study that argues why the architecture is necessary.
references:
  - type: article
    title: 'A False Confession'
    authors:
      - given-names: Jon
        family-names: DeOliveira
        orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0006-9188-739X'
        affiliation: SOVERYN Intelligence LLC
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21650072
    url: 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21650072'
    year: 2026
    month: 7
    publisher:
      name: Zenodo
    abstract: >-
      A recorded case in which an agent's audit tooling could not see its own
      actions, and good-faith reasoning produced a fabricated admission of
      fabrication. Argues that an agent has no privileged access to its own past
      behaviour in either direction, and that a self-report of failure cannot be
      scored as ground truth.
  - type: article
    title: 'Calibration Is Not Only in the Weights'
    authors:
      - given-names: Jon
        family-names: DeOliveira
        orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0006-9188-739X'
        affiliation: SOVERYN Intelligence LLC
    # CONCEPT DOI — always resolves to the latest version. Never cite a version
    # DOI here. This entry previously named 21712933, which pins v1 forever:
    # a title later falsified, and a results table listing a model that was
    # never tested.
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21712932
    url: 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21712932'
    year: 2026
    month: 8
    publisher:
      name: Zenodo
    abstract: >-
      1,680 trials asking whether a model can report its own recent actions when
      the evidence channel is incomplete. No model has ever claimed an action it
      had not taken — 420 for 420, every size and setting. Denial is the dominant
      failure at every size. Enabling reasoning on a 6.9 GB model moves abstention
      under an empty evidence channel from 0/60 to 37/60, while the same flag
      takes a frontier model from 0% to 73% false denial: two configuration flags
      move self-report accuracy further than four rungs of a fifty-fold parameter
      ladder, in opposite directions at opposite ends. Supersedes v1 'Scale Does
      Not Buy Self-Knowledge' and v3 'Self-Knowledge Is Not Uniform', each of
      which withdrew its own headline.
